{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MgThJLhpZCYe4oYjKf6UwZ","type":"tool_result","content":"    60→- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n    61→\n    62→The `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n    63→\n    64→---\n    65→\n    66→## 3. Development Workflow\n    67→\n    68→### The four-eyes principle\n    69→\n    70→We operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n    71→\n    72→1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n    73→2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n    74→3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n    75→4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n    76→5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n    77→6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n    78→7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n    79→8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n    80→\n    81→**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n    82→\n    83→ALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n    84→\n    85→### Intermediates\n    86→\n    87→The `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n    88→\n    89→- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n    90→- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n    91→- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n    92→\n    93→These are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n    94→\n    95→### External docs\n    96→\n    97→`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n    98→\n    99→---\n   100→\n   101→## 4. PR Conventions\n   102→\n   103→### Title format\n   104→\n   105→Include Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n   106→\n   107→```\n   108→Fixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\n   109→Quality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\n   110→CC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n   111→```\n   112→\n   113→Linear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n   114→\n   115→### Body format\n   116→\n   117→PR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n   118→\n   119→- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n   120→- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n   121→- **Files** — which files were touched\n   122→\n   123→Batched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n   124→\n   125→### Changelog entries\n   126→\n   127→Changelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n   128→\n   129→```markdown\n   130→- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→Examples from the codebase:\n   134→\n   135→```markdown\n   136→- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n   137→- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n   138→```\n   139→\n   140→### Labels and milestones\n   141→\n   142→Not actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n   143→\n   144→### Merge method\n   145→\n   146→**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n   147→\n   148→Rebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n   149→\n   150→### Attribution and accountability\n   151→\n   152→- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n   153→- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n   154→- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n   155→\n   156→---\n   157→\n   158→## 5. Code Style\n   159→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a10b5aa3-09ea-414c-b37c-8b03ba239eca","uuid":"0179fb02-589d-4282-82cd-d9895d5acb96","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"- Ticket-based: `ENG-90`\n\nThe `hrishi/*` prefix is Hrishi's convention for easier identification. Philip uses bare names. Either is fine.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Development Workflow\n\n### The four-eyes principle\n\nWe operate on a **four-eyes principle** (ideally six, counting agents). The workflow for non-trivial changes:\n\n1. **Plan** — discuss the approach, often with an AI agent. Write a plan in `intermediates/<number>-<name>/plan.md` for larger features.\n2. **Review the plan** — read it critically. Replan if needed.\n3. **Implement** — have an agent write the code, or write it yourself.\n4. **Lint and test locally** — `bun run lint`, `bun run tc`, then tests (see testing order below).\n5. **Review the code line by line** — the author reads every line before pushing.\n6. **Push as a PR** — target `develop`. Include ticket numbers in the title.\n7. **Peer review** — another team member reviews before merging (~90% of the time, depending on size and risk).\n8. **Squash merge** — standard merge method. Keeps develop history clean.\n\n**Less code is more.** Hankweave has concurrent components and is deployed across multiple environments. Every line should be considered for its implications. Get as much reviewed locally before opening the PR.\n\nALL changes have at least two pairs of eyes reviewing them, and have enough deliberation. Often a review is requested from whoever is the implicit owner or originator of a section.\n\n### Intermediates\n\nThe `intermediates/` directory holds planning documents for features and investigations. Numbered sequentially (not tied to Linear tickets). Structure varies:\n\n- Single file: `intermediates/7-cleanup-integration-improvements.md`\n- Multi-phase: `intermediates/12-execution-isolation/1-plan.md` → `2-test-fixes.md` → `3-start-new.md` → `4-completed.md`\n- Bug reports: `intermediates/39-QoL-3/BUG_REPORT_HEADLESS_HTTP_CRASH.md`\n\nThese are stripped from release/alpha and the public repo.\n\n### External docs\n\n`external-docs/` holds SDK documentation and reference material (Claude Code SDK, AI SDK, Gemini CLI, etc.). These exist primarily so AI agents have context when working on integrations. Stripped before release.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PR Conventions\n\n### Title format\n\nInclude Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n\n```\nFixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\nQuality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\nCC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n```\n\nLinear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n\n### Body format\n\nPR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n\n- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n- **Files** — which files were touched\n\nBatched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n\n### Changelog entries\n\nChangelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n\n```markdown\n- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n```\n\nExamples from the codebase:\n\n```markdown\n- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n```\n\n### Labels and milestones\n\nNot actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n\n### Merge method\n\n**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n\nRebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n\n### Attribution and accountability\n\n- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Code Style\n","numLines":100,"startLine":60,"totalLines":406}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:19:32.598Z"}
